WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.700 --> 00:00:02.963
A hundred and eighteen kinds of atom.

2
00:00:03.063 --> 00:00:04.796
Let's see how they build everything.

3
00:00:09.799 --> 00:00:17.971
This is plutonium. 94 protons crammed into one nucleus, so unstable that it keeps spitting out alpha particles.

4
00:00:18.670 --> 00:00:25.631
It's just one of 118 kinds of atom, and everything in the universe is built from them. Here they all are.

5
00:00:26.231 --> 00:00:35.057
Colour the table by how each pure element bonds: a huge block of metals, molecules on the right, and the noble gases standing alone.

6
00:00:35.658 --> 00:00:43.422
Or by electronegativity: how hard an atom pulls on electrons. Fluorine, top right, is the greediest of all.

7
00:00:44.121 --> 00:00:53.520
Pick sodium. Its electrons fill shells from the inside out: two, then eight, then one lonely outer electron that it gives away easily.

8
00:00:54.319 --> 00:01:03.390
Some elements have more than one face. Pure carbon can be diamond, graphite, a single sheet of graphene, or a football-shaped buckyball.

9
00:01:04.191 --> 00:01:10.886
Sulfur forms rings of eight atoms. Melt it, and the rings break open into long, tangled chains.

10
00:01:11.685 --> 00:01:20.513
Cool helium to two degrees above absolute zero, and it turns superfluid: it flows with zero friction, and even creeps up the walls.

11
00:01:21.314 --> 00:01:29.794
In a metal, outer electrons roam freely as a shared sea. Apply a voltage, and they drift together. That's electric current.

12
00:01:30.793 --> 00:01:39.578
Atoms bond using those outer electrons. Sodium hands its spare one to chlorine, and the two ions lock into a crystal of salt.

13
00:01:40.377 --> 00:01:47.851
Oxygen shares electrons with hydrogen instead, making water: a bent molecule with a slightly negative end.

14
00:01:48.850 --> 00:01:57.795
And the table isn't finished. Element 120 is being hunted right now. It would be the heaviest atom ever made, and start a brand-new row.

15
00:01:58.795 --> 00:02:08.302
Build your own atoms, too. Carbon-14 has two extra neutrons, so it slowly decays, and that slow decay lets us date ancient bones.

16
00:02:09.103 --> 00:02:19.965
Uranium-235 packs 92 protons, all pushing apart. It's barely holding on, which is exactly why it can split, and power a reactor.

17
00:02:23.466 --> 00:02:25.869
Electrons decide how atoms behave.

18
00:02:25.968 --> 00:02:28.171
Bonds share or swap those electrons.

19
00:02:28.271 --> 00:02:30.981
And the nucleus decides if an atom lasts.

20
00:02:30.866 --> 00:02:33.304
Next time: molecules in motion.
